report=f'''STATUS: DONE
COMMIT: none
CHANGED: {count} investigation evidence/source files, plus generated disposable fixtures; 0 production files
OPEN: 0 unresolved required experiments at this checkpoint
BLOCKERS: none for evidence delivery; root instructed no new experiments after separate agent-runtime content-flag error
CONFIRMED: 2
LIMITS: macOS APFS/HFS+; publication finding proved at public store API with explicit same-inode input, not ordinary Cargo recurrence; no Windows/Linux runtime, races or crash tests.
---

Baseline is {sha}. Existing CLI and rlib came from the coordinator baseline; source production stayed unchanged. SHA-256 provenance is in evidence/provenance.txt. No builds above 2 GiB were needed; fixtures and retained HFS+ images total under 200 MiB. HFS+ image mounts were beneath investigation/fixtures, and every successfully mounted image was detached in finally; successful detach commands are in evidence/permission-replay.log.

Two underlying defects were established by executed baseline red assertions. findings.json contains their full test source, production wiring, evidence and impact drivers. cases.json supplies argv, workdir, timeout_seconds, expected_failure_regex and assertion_regex for mechanical replay.

Literal dep-info marker collision is end-to-end through the ordinary compiler wrappers. A Rust crate whose source is __kache_cwd__/main.rs and whose cwd contains a decoy main.rs first prints 1. After removing target and restoring from cache, .d records refer to the decoy rather than the real source. Changing the real source to print 2 produces Cargo Fresh and runtime output 1. The same result was executed for __kache_workspace__, __kache_root__ and __kache_target_rule__; root/target variants supply a target/main.rs decoy. The C++ fixture includes __kache_root__/value.h. Cold dep-info names that real header; restored dep-info names project/value.h. After changing the true header from VALUE 1 to 2, Make skips compilation and runtime output stays 1. See evidence/cwd-replay.log, workspace-replay.log, root-replay.log, target-replay.log, cpp-marker-replay.log and the corresponding result JSON and full command logs. Normal directory controls for both languages rebuild and print 2; they are the golden green partner proving the final correctness assertion distinguishes ordinary behavior.

Duplicate shared publication fails at the production ArtifactStore public API, linked against the untouched baseline rlib on a real generated HFS+ volume. The probe uses immutable .rlib policy, matching the real compiler policy's hardlink allowance; it does not replace filesystem operations or force a private test hook. First publication shares source/blob inode and yields mode 0444. Putting that identical, already-shared source again yields 0666. fs::write(source) succeeds and changes blob bytes. The assertion PUBLISHED_BLOB_BECAME_WRITABLE fails. The existing-blob return in publish_staged_blob_with calls discard_staged_blob, whose unlink_blob clears readonly on the still-shared inode; publish returns without re-arming the published blob. evidence/permissions-public-api.log records actual modes, inode/link count and content consequence. This proves a conditional integrity defect at the exported store boundary. The ordinary Rust wrapper calls the same put function with compiler-owned immutable artifact paths, but no ordinary Cargo workflow reaching a repeated submission of that exact existing blob inode was demonstrated. An exploratory Cargo cold/restore run on the small HFS+ volume produced no blobs; its hfs-cold/hfs-restore logs are retained and do not support a cache-hit claim. Typical recurrence frequency, Linux runtime reproduction and downstream validation after mutation were not measured.

Public materialization controls exercised Hardlink, Copy and ExecutableHardlink on APFS and real HFS+ with 0444 and 0555 sources. All retained original bytes and readonly source state. HFS+ eligible hardlinks rejected writes through shared outputs; private clones/copies accepted writes without source changes. Public writable C/C++ staging and transformed dep-info were writable and independent. Independent publication controls on both filesystems retained mode 0444 after repeated put and source mutation. evidence/permissions-apfs-control.log and permissions-hfs-independent-control.log contain RESTORE_CONTROLS_GREEN and INDEPENDENT_BLOB_PROTECTED and exited 0. These checks dismiss general copy/clone restoration corruption in the sampled paths; they do not erase the duplicate shared-publication red.

All compiler, Cargo, Make, replay binary and hdiutil children received fixture-only HOME, KACHE_CONFIG, KACHE_CACHE_DIR, KACHE_RUNTIME_DIR, CARGO_HOME, TMPDIR/TMP/TEMP. Real RUSTUP_HOME was preserved for the installed toolchain. The shared environment helper asserts resolved paths are beneath investigation before compiler/cache operations. Commands, working directories, fixture environments and stdout/stderr are recorded in per-step evidence logs; permission-replay.log records hdiutil commands and successful detach. Generated fixture source is retained under fixtures/ and complete reusable reproduction source is in restoration-replay.py, cpp-marker-replay.py, permission-replay.py and permission-probe.rs. The inherited prior-sentinel-replay.py is only a prior lead and was not executed.

Structural exploration used the installed codebase-memory skill and the read-only coordinator graph. The graph was ready but Rust semantic coverage was partial, with unresolved calls and unavailable binding/expansion/impl facts. Positive graph hits for link_to_target, writable preparation, publication and marker rewriting were corroborated in source. coverage.json describes bounded source scope and platform limits; graph absence is not treated as proof of no consumers. No full test suite, mutation run, Linux/Windows filesystem run, broad race or crash test was performed.

This is an evidence checkpoint. Root instructed stopping new experiments after the separate agent-runtime error “Agent errored: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk.” No tool automatic-approval rejection blocked these saved experiments. All findings here use completed experiments; that runtime error does not itself establish or refute either defect.
'''
